INT. AGING CAVE - ALPINE CHEESE VAULT - NIGHT

Carved stone. Wheels of cheese on spruce shelves, rows upon rows, rind blooming white and grey. A single gas lamp. The air tastes of salt and earth.

MARGUERITE LOUVEL, 40s, immaculate trench coat, strolls the aisle with her hands behind her back. She might be visiting an aunt.

JUNIOR TWOMBLY, 30s, safecracker, sweater too warm for the cave, kneels at a steel door set into the rock. He listens, ear to the dial.

DELPHINE "DEL" WRAY, 20s, getaway driver, sits cross-legged on a barrel, a thermos on her knee. She watches the entrance.

OSCAR PELLEGRINI-OKAFOR, 50s, the inside man, wears a cheesemonger's smock and sweats through it. He hovers by the door and checks his watch every few seconds.

MARGUERITE
How long?

JUNIOR
Ten minutes.

OSCAR
He said ten minutes at midnight.

MARGUERITE
Oscar. Breathe.

OSCAR
I am breathing. I'm breathing very hard.

DEL
He said that ten minutes ago.

JUNIOR
He is a patient man.

MARGUERITE
Del, the road?

DEL
Quiet. Too quiet.

Marguerite touches a wheel of cheese. Leaves a fingerprint in the rind. She looks at it. Wipes it carefully.

MARGUERITE
Gloves.

JUNIOR
I'm wearing gloves.

MARGUERITE
Del.

DEL
(holds up hands)
Bare. Sorry.

She pulls on gloves. Junior turns the dial. A click. Another.

A shape detaches itself from the shadow between shelves.

A MAN IN A WHITE APRON, 60s, holds a cheese iron like a sword.

MAN IN WHITE APRON
You're in my cave.

Everyone freezes. Del slides off the barrel. Marguerite doesn't flinch.

MARGUERITE
We're expected.

MAN IN WHITE APRON
By who?

MARGUERITE
By the cheese.

His eyes narrow. The cheese iron drops a few inches.

JUNIOR
(whisper)
Marguerite.

MARGUERITE
(quietly, to him)
Keep dialing.

MAN IN WHITE APRON
What do you want?

MARGUERITE
The Reserve. 1978.

He studies her. She holds his eyes. The lamp hisses.

BERTRAND
I am Bertrand Blanchet. I have been guarding that wheel for forty years.

MARGUERITE
Then you know it was never meant to be sold.

BERTRAND
It was not.

DEL
Mr. Blanchet, with respect, it's two million dollars of dairy.

BERTRAND
It is two million dollars of my wife.

Silence. The lamp. A drip somewhere.

Junior stops turning the dial.

JUNIOR
Marguerite. Your call.

Marguerite takes a long breath. Walks toward Bertrand. Stops an arm's length away.

MARGUERITE
What if we only take half?

BERTRAND
Half?

MARGUERITE
You keep half. We sell half. You buy a new roof.

BERTRAND
The roof is fine.

DEL
It's not, though. I saw the leak by the entrance.

BERTRAND
That is a decorative leak.

JUNIOR
Is anybody else confused?

MARGUERITE
Quiet, Junior.

She holds out her gloved hand. Bertrand looks at it. Lowers the cheese iron.

BERTRAND
Half.

He shakes. The safe door clicks open behind them.

Junior pulls it wide. Inside, nestled in straw: a single, enormous, dark wheel.

Everyone stares. Del lifts her thermos.

DEL
To the wife.

They raise nothing, because they have nothing. Bertrand takes the thermos, drinks, hands it back.
